.TH CRPAIC_MIN 3 "2025-07-22" "CRpaic" "CRpaic User's Manual"
.SH NAME
crpaic_min - return the lesser of two numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <CRpaic.h>
.PP
.BI "TYPE crpaic_min (" n1 ", " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "         char crpaic_min_c (char const " n1 ", char const " n2 ");"
.BI "unsigned char crpaic_min_uc (unsigned char const " n1 ","
.BI "                             unsigned char const " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "         short int crpaic_min_si (short int const " n1 ","
.BI "                                  short int const " n2 ");"
.BI "unsigned short int crpaic_min_usi (unsigned short int const " n1 ","
.BI "                                   unsigned short int const " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "         int crpaic_min_i (int const " n1 ", int const " n2 ");"
.BI "unsigned int crpaic_min_ui (unsigned int const " n1 ","
.BI "                            unsigned int const " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "         long int crpaic_min_li (long int const " n1 ","
.BI "                                 long int const " n2 ");"
.BI "unsigned long int crpaic_min_uli (unsigned long int const " n1 ","
.BI "                                  unsigned long int const " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "         long long int crpaic_min_lli (long long int const " n1 ","
.BI "                                       long long int const " n2 ");"
.BI "unsigned long long int crpaic_min_ulli ("
.BI "    unsigned long long int const " n1 ","
.BI "    unsigned long long int const " n2 ");"
.PP
.BI "float crpaic_min_f (float const " f1 ", float const " f2 ");"
.PP
.BI "double crpaic_min_d (double const " d1 ", double const " d2 ");"
.PP
.BI "long double crpaic_min_ld (long double const " ld1 ","
.BI "                           long double const " ld2 ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR crpaic_min ()
function is a family of functions that takes as arguments two numbers
.IR n1
and
.IR n2
(of any integer of floating-point data type) and return the lesser of the two.
The user can pass the following types of arguments:
.PP
    a) Signed or unsigned integers: char, short int, int, long int,
       long long int, int8_t, uint8_t, int16_t, uint16_t, int32_t, uint32_t,
       int64_t, uint64_t;
.PP
    b) Floating-point numbers: float, double, long double.
.PP
The
.BR crpaic_min ()
function is a _Generic macro that dispatches the arguments
.IR n1
and
.IR n2
to the correct specific function depending on the data type of
.IR n1
(the first argument). The client program can directly use one of the specific
functions to a given data type but, in general, it is recommended to use only
the generic
.BR crpaic_min ()
macro in cliente programs.
.PP
The user MUST pass two arguments of the same type (or, at least, two
arguments of compatible types, eg.: int and int32_t). If the user passed two
arguments of different types, the behavior would be undefined and the return
value could be wrong (due to C's type promotion and casting rules). To
prevent this, the _Generic dispatch macro verify the type compatibility of
the arguments and stop the compilation if the types are different (or not
compatible).
.PP
Functions specific to a given data type are indicated by a standardized suffix,
for example,
.BR crpaic_min_si ()
takes two short int as arguments;
.BR crpaic_min_uli ()
takes two unsigned long int as arguments. OK, I know that embedding the
argument\(aqs data type in the function name (know as \(dqHungarian
Notation\(dq) is stupid. However, due to the purely academic nature of the
CRpaic library and to the use of the generic dispatch macro that works for any
numeric data type (integer and floating-point), I don\(aqt see any major
problems with this practice.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR crpaic_min ()
return the lesser of two numbers. The TYPE of the return value will always be
the same type as the first argument; that is, the type of the first argument,
.IR n1 ,
determines the type of the returned value.
.SH ERRORS
.BR crpaic_min ()
does not set any
.IR errno
even in the event of an error.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
.BR crpaic_min ()
is Reentrant and Thread-Safe (Multi Thread-Save; MT-Safe). For more explanation
about these concepts, see
.BR attributes (7).
.SH CONFORMING TO
Not applicable.
.SH EXAMPLE
.in +4n
.EX
#include <CRpaic.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main (void)
{
    long int i = 10;
    int64_t j = 20;

    /* long int and int64_t are compatible types, the compilation can be done */
    long int n = crpaic_min(i, j);

    /* print 10 */
    printf(\(dq%ld\\n\(dq, n);

    /* some doubles */
    double d1 = 30.0;
    double d2 = 50.0;

    /* same data type, not problem */
    double r = crpaic_min(d1, d2);

    /* print 30.00 */
    printf(\(dq%.2f\\n\(dq, r);
    
    return 0;
}
.EE
.in
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The arguments 
.IR n1
and
.IR n2
can be of virtually any numeric data types (integer or floating-point), but the
user must ensure that their types are the same or, at least, compatible. It\(aqs
best to use the generic dispatch macro,
.BR crpaic_min (),
instead of using the specific functions.
.SH RATIONALE
Determining the lesser of two numbers is an extremely common operation in
programming, and therefore,
.BR crpaic_min ()
can be used in a wide range of computational problems.
